1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,727
That hole wasn't made by a truck. It was dug from underneath, by water.

2
00:00:05,897 --> 00:00:09,977
The secret: a pothole is hollow long before it opens.

3
00:00:10,147 --> 00:00:15,411
It starts with a hairline crack. Rain slips through, into the gravel below.

4
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:22,128
Below zero Celsius, thirty-two Fahrenheit, that water freezes and grows by nine percent.

5
00:00:22,298 --> 00:00:27,055
The ice pushes up and lifts the asphalt, like a fist under a blanket.

6
00:00:27,225 --> 00:00:31,879
It even pulls more water up from the soil, so the bump keeps growing.

7
00:00:32,049 --> 00:00:36,405
Then it thaws. The ice melts, but the asphalt doesn't fall back.

8
00:00:36,575 --> 00:00:41,102
Now there's an empty pocket under a thin lid of road, over soggy mud.

9
00:00:41,272 --> 00:00:45,852
A tire rolls over. The lid has nothing under it, so it snaps.

10
00:00:46,022 --> 00:00:52,416
Every freeze and thaw repeats it. That's why potholes bloom in early spring, not deep winter.

11
00:00:52,586 --> 00:00:57,263
And a patch dropped into a wet hole? The water's still there. It comes back.

12
00:00:57,433 --> 00:01:01,373
The truck only breaks the lid. The water dug the hole.

13
00:01:01,543 --> 00:01:07,442
Cogwise: how things work. Follow for the next one!
